{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-127091",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-6{x}^{2}+12x+18=-6(x+1)(x-3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the two critical points $x=-1$ and $x=3.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-2,0,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-2) & =-6(-2+1)(-2-3)=-30<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-6(0+1)(0-3)=18>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(4) & =-6(4+1)(4-3)=-30<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-2$ |  | $0$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n* At $x=-1,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-1$ is a local minimum.\n\n* At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
